the knowledge base div36 includes:

%% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% PART 1 - THE GATE: exempt income, or non-assessable non-exempt income?
%%
%% This is the distinction the whole of LodgeiT ticket #56568 turned on and that
%% nobody in the thread established. Exempt income reduces tax losses. NANE does
%% not. Everything downstream is conditional on this.
%%
%% ITAA 1997 s 6-20 (Exempt income), s 6-23 (Non-assessable non-exempt income).
%% Compilation C2026C00324, 27 Aug 2026, Volume 1, Part 1-3, Division 6.
%% https://www.legislation.gov.au/C2004A05138/latest/text
%% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

% s 6-20: ordinary or statutory income is exempt income if made exempt from
% income tax by a provision of this Act or another Commonwealth law.
a receipt is exempt income
    if the receipt is made exempt from income tax by a provision.

% s 6-23: an amount is non-assessable non-exempt income where a provision states
% it is not assessable income and is not exempt income. NANE is not exempt
% income and never enters s 36-10 or s 36-20.
a receipt is non assessable non exempt income
    if the receipt is made not assessable and not exempt by a provision.

% A receipt whose character has not been established. The model must not guess.
a receipt is of undetermined character
    if a taxpayer derived the receipt in a year
    and it is not the case that
        the receipt is exempt income
    and it is not the case that
        the receipt is non assessable non exempt income
    and it is not the case that
        the receipt is included in assessable income by a provision.

% The loud-failure gate. While any receipt is uncharacterised, net exempt income
% below is not derivable and no carry-forward figure can be produced at all.
a taxpayer has characterised every receipt in a year
    if the taxpayer has total deductions of a guard in the year
    and it is not the case that
        the taxpayer derived a receipt in the year
        and the receipt is of undetermined character.

%% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% PART 2 - NET EXEMPT INCOME
%%
%% ITAA 1997 s 36-20(1): for an Australian resident, net exempt income is the
%% amount by which total exempt income from all sources exceeds the total of
%% (a) the losses and outgoings (except capital) incurred in deriving that
%% exempt income, and (b) any taxes payable outside Australia on it.
%%
%% "The amount by which X exceeds Y" is nil, not a negative number, where Y is
%% the greater - hence the maximum against 0.
%% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

a taxpayer has gross exempt income of an amount in a year
    if the taxpayer has total deductions of a guard in the year
    and the amount is the sum of each partial such that
        the taxpayer derived a receipt in the year
        and the receipt is exempt income
        and the receipt is an amount of the partial.

% s 36-20(1)(a). This is the gross-versus-net question from the ticket. Every
% dollar here moves the final carry-forward figure by a dollar.
a taxpayer has exempt outgoings of an amount in a year
    if the taxpayer has total deductions of a guard in the year
    and the amount is the sum of each partial such that
        the taxpayer derived a receipt in the year
        and the receipt is exempt income
        and an outgoing was incurred by the taxpayer in deriving the receipt
        and the outgoing is an amount of the partial.

% s 36-20(1)(b).
a taxpayer has foreign tax on exempt income of an amount in a year
    if the taxpayer has total deductions of a guard in the year
    and the amount is the sum of each partial such that
        the taxpayer derived a receipt in the year
        and the receipt is exempt income
        and the taxpayer paid foreign tax of the partial on the receipt.

a taxpayer has net exempt income of an amount in a year
    if the taxpayer has characterised every receipt in the year
    and the taxpayer has gross exempt income of a gross in the year
    and the taxpayer has exempt outgoings of an outgoings in the year
    and the taxpayer has foreign tax on exempt income of a foreign impost in the year
    and a surplus = gross - outgoings - foreign impost
    and the maximum of the surplus and 0 is the amount.

%% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% PART 3 - THE CURRENT YEAR TAX LOSS
%%
%% ITAA 1997 s 36-10, method statement:
%%   Step 1  add up the amounts you can deduct (except earlier-year tax losses)
%%   Step 2  subtract your total assessable income
%%   Step 3  if you derived exempt income, ALSO subtract your net exempt income
%%   Step 4  any amount REMAINING is your tax loss for the income year
%%
%% Step 3 is unconditional and sits INSIDE the loss calculation. Step 4 means a
%% tax loss exists only to the extent an amount remains: where net exempt income
%% equals or exceeds the excess, the tax loss is nil - not reduced, not deferred.
%%
%% Step 3 is the step the LodgeiT engine omits (#56568). It is also the step that
%% must NOT be added on its own - see PART 6.
%% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

% Steps 1 and 2.
a taxpayer has an excess of deductions of an amount in a year
    if the taxpayer has total deductions of a deductions in the year
    and the taxpayer has total assessable income of an assessable in the year
    and a shortfall = deductions - assessable
    and the maximum of the shortfall and 0 is the amount.

% Steps 3 and 4.
a taxpayer has a tax loss of an amount for a year
    if the taxpayer has an excess of deductions of an excess in the year
    and the taxpayer has net exempt income of a net exempt in the year
    and a residue = excess - net exempt
    and the maximum of the residue and 0 is the amount.

%% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% PART 4 - DEDUCTING EARLIER-YEAR LOSSES IN A DEFICIT YEAR
%%
%% ITAA 1997 s 36-15(4), entities other than corporate tax entities:
%%   "However, if you have net exempt income for the later income year and those
%%    deductions exceed your total assessable income, then: (a) subtract that
%%    excess from your net exempt income; and (b) deduct the tax loss from any
%%    net exempt income that remains."
%%
%% ITAA 1997 s 36-17(4) is the corporate tax entity analogue and is materially
%% identical on this limb. The "as the entity chooses" latitude in s 36-17
%% attaches to deduction against ASSESSABLE income - subsections (2) and (3)(b) -
%% and does not reach the net exempt income limb. So entity type does not change
%% the answer. Scenario 'company' below proves that mechanically.
%%
%% Paragraph (a) is the step every draft on #56568 skipped, Jenny's included -
%% she reached the right number by the right route but cited only s 36-15.
%% It is what stops the exempt income being spent twice.
%% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

% s 36-15(4)(a) - individuals and other non-corporate entities.
a taxpayer has net exempt income remaining of an amount in a year
    if the taxpayer is an individual
    and the taxpayer has an excess of deductions of an excess in the year
    and the excess is greater than 0
    and the taxpayer has net exempt income of a net exempt in the year
    and a remainder = net exempt - excess
    and the maximum of the remainder and 0 is the amount.

% s 36-17(4)(a) - corporate tax entities. Same mechanic, different provision.
a taxpayer has net exempt income remaining of an amount in a year
    if the taxpayer is a corporate tax entity
    and the taxpayer has an excess of deductions of an excess in the year
    and the excess is greater than 0
    and the taxpayer has net exempt income of a net exempt in the year
    and a remainder = net exempt - excess
    and the maximum of the remainder and 0 is the amount.

% s 36-15(4)(b) / s 36-17(4)(b) - deduct the tax loss from what remains.
a taxpayer absorbs an amount of the loss from a loss year in a year
    if the taxpayer has an undeducted tax loss of a brought forward from the loss year
    and the taxpayer has net exempt income remaining of a remaining in the year
    and the minimum of the brought forward and the remaining is the amount.

%% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% PART 5 - PROFIT YEAR (regression guard)
%%
%% ITAA 1997 s 36-15(3): where there is net exempt income and total assessable
%% income exceeds total deductions, the tax loss is deducted (a) first from net
%% exempt income and (b) secondly from the excess of assessable income.
%%
%% Included deliberately. In a profit year the ATO's L1 Worksheet 1 rule
%% "row g = row a - row f" IS a correct proxy for the statute and the LodgeiT
%% engine is correct. The #56568 defect is bounded to the deficit case, and any
%% fix must leave this branch alone.
%% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

a taxpayer has net exempt income remaining of an amount in a year
    if the taxpayer has an excess of deductions of 0 in the year
    and the taxpayer has net exempt income of the amount in the year.

%% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% PART 6 - CARRY FORWARD AND THE CONSERVATION INVARIANT
%%
%% The invariant is the point of this file. Exempt income consumed across the
%% two steps must equal the lesser of net exempt income and total capacity to
%% absorb it (the current-year excess plus the losses brought forward):
%%
%%     consumed  =  min(NEI, excess)          [ s 36-10 Step 3 ]
%%               +  amounts absorbed          [ s 36-15(4)(b) ]
%%     ceiling   =  min(NEI, excess + prior losses)
%%
%% On the #56568 figures (NEI 4579, excess 2822, prior losses 3286):
%%     correct     2822 + 1757 = 4579 of 4579   consistent
%%     engine      0    + 3286 = 3286 of 4579   under - Step 3 never applied
%%     naive fix   2822 + 3286 = 6108 of 4579   over  - counted twice, gives 0
%%
%% The naive fix - adding s 36-10 Step 3 while leaving the L1 worksheet's
%% "row g = row a - row f" untouched - satisfies the customer's complaint (the
%% number goes down) and breaks this invariant. Both steps must change together.
%% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

a taxpayer has total undeducted losses of an amount before a year
    if the taxpayer has total deductions of a guard in the year
    and the amount is the sum of each partial such that
        the taxpayer has an undeducted tax loss of the partial from a loss year.

a taxpayer carries forward an amount of the loss from a loss year after a year
    if the taxpayer has an undeducted tax loss of a brought forward from the loss year
    and the taxpayer absorbs an absorbed of the loss from the loss year in the year
    and the amount = brought forward - absorbed.

a taxpayer carries forward total losses of an amount after a year
    if the taxpayer has a tax loss of a year loss for the year
    and a prior total is the sum of each partial such that
        the taxpayer carries forward the partial of the loss from a loss year after the year
    and the amount = year loss + prior total.

a taxpayer consumes exempt income of an amount in a year
    if the taxpayer has net exempt income of a net exempt in the year
    and the taxpayer has an excess of deductions of an excess in the year
    and the minimum of the net exempt and the excess is a deficit part
    and a prior part is the sum of each partial such that
        the taxpayer absorbs the partial of the loss from a loss year in the year
    and the amount = deficit part + prior part.

a taxpayer applies net exempt income consistently in a year
    if the taxpayer has net exempt income of a net exempt in the year
    and the taxpayer has an excess of deductions of an excess in the year
    and the taxpayer has total undeducted losses of a prior losses before the year
    and a capacity = excess + prior losses
    and the minimum of the net exempt and the capacity is a ceiling
    and the taxpayer consumes exempt income of a consumed in the year
    and the consumed is equal to the ceiling.

%% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% SCENARIOS
%%
%% Figures are those of LodgeiT support ticket #56568 (Freshdesk, 29-31 Aug 2026,
%% Natural Numbers Accounting). The taxpayer is fictional; the numbers are the
%% ticket's:
%%     losses brought forward   3286
%%     current year deficit     2822   (deductions less assessable income)
%%     exempt income            4579
%% The engine returned 2822. Jenny's manual answer was 1529.
%% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

% -- A: the ticket as reported, receipt characterised as exempt income and no
% related outgoings. No current-year tax loss survives; 1529 carried forward.
scenario ticket is:
    Wren Kavanagh is an individual.
    Wren Kavanagh has total deductions of 2822 in FY2026.
    Wren Kavanagh has total assessable income of 0 in FY2026.
    Wren Kavanagh has an undeducted tax loss of 3286 from FY2025.
    Wren Kavanagh derived the Kavanagh pension in FY2026.
    the Kavanagh pension is an amount of 4579.
    the Kavanagh pension is made exempt from income tax by section 52-10.
    net exempt income expects answers ["Wren Kavanagh has net exempt income of 4579 in FY2026"].
    current year loss expects answers ["Wren Kavanagh has a tax loss of 0 for FY2026"].
    carried forward expects answers ["Wren Kavanagh carries forward total losses of 1529 after FY2026"].
    conservation expects answers ["Wren Kavanagh applies net exempt income consistently in FY2026"].
    missing facts expects answers [].
    engine answer expects answers [].

% -- B: the same receipt characterised as NANE instead. No offset arises
% anywhere. The current-year loss survives in full and the prior year loss is
% untouched. If the ticket's 4579 turns out to be NANE, this is the answer and
% the engine's 2822 is still wrong - just wrong by more.
scenario nane is:
    Wren Kavanagh is an individual.
    Wren Kavanagh has total deductions of 2822 in FY2026.
    Wren Kavanagh has total assessable income of 0 in FY2026.
    Wren Kavanagh has an undeducted tax loss of 3286 from FY2025.
    Wren Kavanagh derived the Kavanagh receipt in FY2026.
    the Kavanagh receipt is an amount of 4579.
    the Kavanagh receipt is made not assessable and not exempt by section 59-30.
    net exempt income expects answers ["Wren Kavanagh has net exempt income of 0 in FY2026"].
    current year loss expects answers ["Wren Kavanagh has a tax loss of 2822 for FY2026"].
    carried forward expects answers ["Wren Kavanagh carries forward total losses of 6108 after FY2026"].
    conservation expects answers ["Wren Kavanagh applies net exempt income consistently in FY2026"].
    missing facts expects answers [].
    engine answer expects answers [].

% -- C: the gross-versus-net trap. 4579 gross with 2822 of outgoings incurred in
% deriving it, so net exempt income is 1757 - less than the deficit, and nothing
% remains for the prior year loss. Total 4351. This is the figure the third
% internal draft reached, by the wrong route: it used 1757 as the STARTING
% exempt income rather than as a residual. Right method, wrong input.
scenario gross is:
    Wren Kavanagh is an individual.
    Wren Kavanagh has total deductions of 2822 in FY2026.
    Wren Kavanagh has total assessable income of 0 in FY2026.
    Wren Kavanagh has an undeducted tax loss of 3286 from FY2025.
    Wren Kavanagh derived the Kavanagh pension in FY2026.
    the Kavanagh pension is an amount of 4579.
    the Kavanagh pension is made exempt from income tax by section 52-10.
    the pension management fee was incurred by Wren Kavanagh in deriving the Kavanagh pension.
    the pension management fee is an amount of 2822.
    net exempt income expects answers ["Wren Kavanagh has net exempt income of 1757 in FY2026"].
    current year loss expects answers ["Wren Kavanagh has a tax loss of 1065 for FY2026"].
    carried forward expects answers ["Wren Kavanagh carries forward total losses of 4351 after FY2026"].
    conservation expects answers ["Wren Kavanagh applies net exempt income consistently in FY2026"].

% -- D: the silent cohort. Same deficit and same exempt income, but no prior year
% loss to argue about. The s 36-10 Step 3 failure alone overstates the carry
% forward by the whole 2822 and no customer would ever have a figure to dispute.
% This population is larger than the ticket's and nobody has complained.
scenario no prior loss is:
    Wren Kavanagh is an individual.
    Wren Kavanagh has total deductions of 2822 in FY2026.
    Wren Kavanagh has total assessable income of 0 in FY2026.
    Wren Kavanagh derived the Kavanagh pension in FY2026.
    the Kavanagh pension is an amount of 4579.
    the Kavanagh pension is made exempt from income tax by section 52-10.
    net exempt income expects answers ["Wren Kavanagh has net exempt income of 4579 in FY2026"].
    current year loss expects answers ["Wren Kavanagh has a tax loss of 0 for FY2026"].
    carried forward expects answers ["Wren Kavanagh carries forward total losses of 0 after FY2026"].
    conservation expects answers ["Wren Kavanagh applies net exempt income consistently in FY2026"].

% -- E: the loud failure, and the real evidentiary position of #56568 today.
% Identical to scenario A except that the character of the receipt is never
% stated. Nothing about net exempt income, the tax loss or the carry forward is
% derivable. The model refuses to guess, and 'missing facts' names what is
% needed. Two AI analyses, a support team and a dev team all produced a
% confident number from exactly this evidence.
scenario uncharacterised is:
    Wren Kavanagh is an individual.
    Wren Kavanagh has total deductions of 2822 in FY2026.
    Wren Kavanagh has total assessable income of 0 in FY2026.
    Wren Kavanagh has an undeducted tax loss of 3286 from FY2025.
    Wren Kavanagh derived the Kavanagh pension in FY2026.
    the Kavanagh pension is an amount of 4579.
    net exempt income expects answers [].
    current year loss expects answers [].
    carried forward expects answers [].
    missing facts expects answers ["the Kavanagh pension is of undetermined character"].
    engine answer expects answers [].

% -- F: profit year regression guard. Assessable income exceeds deductions, so
% s 36-15(3) governs, the full net exempt income is available against the prior
% year loss, and the whole 3286 is absorbed. The LodgeiT engine is correct in
% this branch. A fix for #56568 must not disturb it.
scenario profit is:
    Wren Kavanagh is an individual.
    Wren Kavanagh has total deductions of 1000 in FY2026.
    Wren Kavanagh has total assessable income of 9000 in FY2026.
    Wren Kavanagh has an undeducted tax loss of 3286 from FY2025.
    Wren Kavanagh derived the Kavanagh pension in FY2026.
    the Kavanagh pension is an amount of 4579.
    the Kavanagh pension is made exempt from income tax by section 52-10.
    net exempt income expects answers ["Wren Kavanagh has net exempt income of 4579 in FY2026"].
    current year loss expects answers ["Wren Kavanagh has a tax loss of 0 for FY2026"].
    carried forward expects answers ["Wren Kavanagh carries forward total losses of 0 after FY2026"].
    conservation expects answers ["Wren Kavanagh applies net exempt income consistently in FY2026"].

% -- G: entity type control. The ticket figures again, but the taxpayer is a
% corporate tax entity, so s 36-17(4) governs instead of s 36-15(4). Same answer,
% 1529. This is the ruling that entity type does not move the number, expressed
% mechanically rather than argued. Note this does NOT test Division 165
% continuity of ownership or business continuity, which are separate questions
% and could independently deny the loss.
scenario company is:
    Trellis Holdings Pty Ltd is a corporate tax entity.
    Trellis Holdings Pty Ltd has total deductions of 2822 in FY2026.
    Trellis Holdings Pty Ltd has total assessable income of 0 in FY2026.
    Trellis Holdings Pty Ltd has an undeducted tax loss of 3286 from FY2025.
    Trellis Holdings Pty Ltd derived the Trellis grant in FY2026.
    the Trellis grant is an amount of 4579.
    the Trellis grant is made exempt from income tax by section 52-10.
    net exempt income expects answers ["Trellis Holdings Pty Ltd has net exempt income of 4579 in FY2026"].
    carried forward expects answers ["Trellis Holdings Pty Ltd carries forward total losses of 1529 after FY2026"].
    conservation expects answers ["Trellis Holdings Pty Ltd applies net exempt income consistently in FY2026"].

%% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% QUERIES
%% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

query net exempt income is:
    which taxpayer has net exempt income of which amount in which year.

query current year loss is:
    which taxpayer has a tax loss of which amount for which year.

query carried forward is:
    which taxpayer carries forward total losses of which amount after which year.

query prior year detail is:
    which taxpayer carries forward which amount of the loss from which loss year after which year.

query conservation is:
    which taxpayer applies net exempt income consistently in which year.

query missing facts is:
    which receipt is of undetermined character.

% The engine's answer. Not derivable from Division 36 under ANY characterisation
% of the receipt: 1529 if exempt income, 6108 if NANE. Never 2822. Expected to
% return no answers in every scenario - that is the defect, stated as a
% falsifiable test rather than an assertion.
query engine answer is:
    Wren Kavanagh carries forward total losses of 2822 after FY2026.
